  "ai_language_tip": "Russian (ru) is the safe working language in Tashkent, Dushanbe and Ashgabat businesses; Uzbek, Tajik and Turkmen are not on Bageo's list, so switch only if the shop clearly prefers Russian-free talk and offer English as a fallback.",
  "english_ok": "rarely — Hotels, international clinics and tour agencies may speak it; markets, garages and most salons do not.",
  "politeness": "Formal вы with strangers, a short greeting before business, and calm courtesy; elders get extra respect, and haggling is normal only at bazaars.",
  "name_tip": "Names are given name + surname, often with Russian-style endings (-ov/-ova); older people may expect the patronymic; spell names letter by letter.",
  "phone_tip": "Uzbek mobiles are 9 digits after +998 (90 123 45 67); Tajik numbers are 9 digits after +992; Turkmen mobiles are 8 digits after +993; read in 2-3-2-2 groups.",
  "time_tip": "24-hour clock everywhere; Tashkent and Dushanbe are UTC+5, as is Ashgabat.",
  "number_tip": "Prices in sum (Uzbekistan), somoni (Tajikistan) or manat (Turkmenistan); sums in Uzbekistan run into thousands, so say 'тысяч' clearly.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid 13:00–14:00 lunch, Friday midday near mosques, and Navruz (21 March) plus Eid days when many places close.",
  "closed_days": "Offices work Monday to Friday, some Saturday; bazaars and shops open daily; Navruz and Eid close many businesses.",
  "booking_channels": "Telegram is the main business chat in Uzbekistan and Tajikistan; payments by Click, Payme or Humo/Uzcard cards in Uzbekistan; taxis via Yandex Go; Turkmenistan relies on phone calls because many apps are blocked.",
  "voice_menu": "Telcos (Beeline, Ucell, Uzmobile, Tcell, TMCELL) and banks run IVR in the local language and Russian.",
  "callback_habits": "Uzbek and Tajik shops confirm in Telegram, often by voice message; in Turkmenistan expect a callback by phone.",
